.\" AXE modifications: document selector defaults, precedence, and limitations.
.SS Three flag conventions, one tool
.PP
Historical \fBps\fR implementations grew three overlapping flag
languages: System V / POSIX uses leading dashes (\fB\-e\fR), BSD
uses no dashes at all (\fBaux\fR), and GNU adds long forms
(\fB\-\-all\fR). The combinations users still type most often are
\fBps \-ef\fR (Unix) and \fBps aux\fR (BSD); both are supported
here. Many of the more obscure flags from upstream \fBps\fR are not
yet implemented — see the limitations note below.
.SS Default selection rule
.PP
Without a selection flag, Unix-style \fBps\fR selects processes with
the caller's effective user ID and controlling terminal. When the
caller has no terminal, matching processes without terminals are
included.
.PP
Bare BSD options change the default to the caller's effective user's
processes on any controlling terminal. \fBa\fR removes the user
restriction, \fBx\fR includes your processes without terminals,
and \fBax\fR selects all processes. \fBT\fR adds the caller's terminal
without narrowing a broader selection; terminal number 0 also matches.
.PP
Explicit PID and user filters combine additively. PID lists replace
the broad selections made by \fB\-e\fR, \fB\-A\fR, \fBa\fR, and
\fBax\fR. Without a PID list, \fB\-e\fR / \fB\-A\fR continues to
select all processes even when user filters are present. The active
process filter \fBr\fR / \fB\-r\fR is applied after these selections.
.PP
The compatibility form \fB\-ax\fR is interpreted as BSD \fBax\fR.
\fB\-au\fR without a user argument uses BSD \fBau\fR; with a user
argument it retains Unix \fB\-a \-u USER\fR semantics.
.SS %CPU is a lifetime average
.PP
The \fI%CPU\fR column under \fBaux\fR is the process's total CPU
time divided by its wall-clock age (in percent of one CPU). A
long-running process that briefly burned 100% then idled averages
out to a low value, and the figure decays as wall-clock time
elapses without further CPU use. This matches historical
\fBps aux\fR semantics.
.PP
Only the process's own CPU time is counted (\fIutime\fR + \fIstime\fR
from \fI/proc/[pid]/stat\fR); time consumed by exited children
(\fIcutime\fR + \fIcstime\fR) is not folded in.
.PP
For a "what's eating CPU right now" view, prefer \fBtop\fR(1),
which reports recent-interval percentages.
.SS Output width and command text
.PP
Output width comes from \fICOLUMNS\fR or the terminal. Redirected
output without \fICOLUMNS\fR has no screen-width bound.
\fBw\fR or \fB\-w\fR widens bounded output to at least 132 columns;
two occurrences, including \fBww\fR, \fB\-ww\fR, or \fBw \-w\fR,
remove the width bound.
.PP
\fB\-\-cols WIDTH\fR, \fB\-\-columns WIDTH\fR, and
\fB\-\-width WIDTH\fR set an explicit width. The last explicit
value wins, then the accumulated \fBw\fR options apply.
Command fields are clipped by display columns without splitting UTF-8.
Fixed fields and headers survive narrow widths. Column padding is
dynamic rather than using procps's fixed minimum widths.
.PP
C and UTF-8 locales are supported. Command-line newlines become
spaces; other control characters, including terminal escapes, become
\fB?\fR. Legacy non-UTF-8 character sets are not emulated.
Nominally unlimited output retains a 128 KiB buffer bound.
.SS Thread snapshots
.PP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-T\fR and BSD \fBH\fR list actual tasks, including
the main thread. PID/TGID remains the process ID; TID/LWP/SPID is
the task ID. Task CPU time, state, name and scheduling come from
\fI/proc/[pid]/task/[tid]/stat\fR. Memory and NLWP belong to the process.
\fB\-p\fR selects process IDs, not nonleader task IDs.
.PP
\fB\-m\fR and BSD \fBm\fR print each process summary followed by
its tasks. Inapplicable fields contain \fB\-\fR. Summary CPU time
is the true process aggregate. Running-only selection applies to
individual tasks in flat modes and to processes in mixed mode.
Tasks that disappear or cannot be read are skipped.
.SS Sorting
.PP
\fB\-\-sort=KEY,\-KEY,...\fR and BSD \fBk\fR accept multiple
case-sensitive keys. A leading \fB+\fR or no sign sorts ascending;
\fB\-\fR sorts descending. Invalid, missing or repeated sort options
exit with status 1.
.PP
Keys use raw values before display rounding, clipping or mixed-row
masking. Numeric IDs sort numerically; user and group names sort as
text. \fIcomm\fR sorts task names; \fIargs\fR, \fIcmd\fR and
\fIcommand\fR sort full command lines. \fIpri\fR sorts kernel priority,
\fI%mem\fR sorts RSS, and \fI%cpu\fR sorts lifetime utilization.
Equal keys retain PID/TID order.
.PP
Flat thread modes sort all tasks together. Mixed mode sorts process
groups and their tasks separately, retaining each summary first.
Sorting preserves every readable task. This avoids task loss in
procps-ng 4.0.6's sorted \fB\-L\fR/\fB\-m\fR output; mixed running-only
selection also terminates when only a worker is active.
\fIvsize\fR sorts actual stat bytes and \fIvsz\fR sorts status VmSize,
rather than copying the local procps-ng 4.0.6's PID-order vsize result.
.SS Limitations
.PP
This implementation is a deliberately small subset of upstream
\fBps\fR. Notable absences:
.IP \(bu 2
No process-group / parent-PID / TTY filters (\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-ppid\fR,
\fB\-t\fR). Use \fBpgrep\fR(1) for richer selection.
.IP \(bu 2
No tree view (\fB\-\-forest\fR). Use \fBpstree\fR(1) for
parent-child hierarchies.
